romanized_response:
HTTP status code 429 Too Many Requests ka matlab hai ki server par kisi tarah ka rate limit hai aur aapki request ne use exceed kar diya hai. Yeh neeche diye gaye kisi bhi reason ki wajah se ho sakta hai:

1. Jo code yeh request karta hai, use thode se time mein bahut baar run kiya gaya, jisse rate limit exceed ho gaya.

2. Website un requests ko 429 status code ke saath reject karti hai jinhe woh spam samajhti hai. Iska matlab ho sakta hai ki user agent koi common browser nahi hai, jo ek simple check hai jo kuch websites use karti hain. Isse theek karne ke liye, aap GET request mein "User-Agent" header is tarah add kar sakte hain:

```
const response = await axios.get('https://www.realestate.com.au/rent/in-sydney,+nsw/list-1', 
     { headers: { 'User-Agent': 'browser.version' }  }
);
```

Dhyan rahe ki aapko `"browser.version"` ko actual browser user agent string se replace karna hoga. Yeh code kis application mein run ho raha hai, is par depend karte hue, ise user agent spoofing mana ja sakta hai, jo kuch countries ya organizations ke laws ke under restricted ho sakta hai (ya us website ki terms of service ke under, jise code access karne ki koshish kar raha hai).

3. Website kisi aur reason se requests ko rate limit kar rahi hai. Yeh request proxy ke through kiye jaane ki wajah se ho sakta hai; is case mein proxy ke doosre users ne shayad bahut zyada requests ki hongi, halanki yeh unlikely hai.

4. Website sabhi requests ko reject kar rahi hai kyunki us par denial of service attack ho raha hai. Yeh unlikely hai, kyunki aise attack ke dauran server ka typical behavior traffic ko kisi non-existent server par route karna, ya use poori tarah deny karna hota hai, jisse koi valid response bilkul nahi milta. Halanki, agar website HTTP status code ke saath respond bhi kare, to woh likely server error 503 Service Unavailable hoga, jo tab diya jata hai jab server overloaded hota hai.

response_en:
Yes, certainly! The issue you're encountering is because the string literals "fizzbuzz", "fizz", and "buzz" are temporary values, and you are trying to return a reference to them, which is not allowed because they will be dropped at the end of the scope, and the returned reference would be dangling.

You can fix this error by returning &'static str instead of &str. &'static str refers to string literals, which have a 'static lifetime, meaning they last for the duration of the program. Here's an updated version of your code:

fn fizzbuzz(n: usize) -> &'static str {
    match (n % 3, n % 5) {
        (0, 0) => "fizzbuzz",
        (0, _) => "fizz",
        (_, 0) => "buzz",
        _ => {
            let s = n.to_string();
            Box::leak(s.into_boxed_str())
        }
    }
}

Note that the last arm returns a &'static str that is created from a String using the Box::leak function, which takes ownership of a Box<T> and returns a pointer to its contents, effectively leaking it. This is safe because the returned pointer will last for the duration of the program, but it should be used with care as it will not be cleaned up automatically.

romanized_response:
Haan, bilkul! Jo issue aapko aa raha hai, woh isliye hai kyunki string literals "fizzbuzz", "fizz", aur "buzz" temporary values hain, aur aap unka reference return karne ki koshish kar rahe hain, jo allowed nahi hai kyunki scope ke end par ye drop ho jayengi, aur returned reference dangling ho jayega.

Aap is error ko &str ki jagah &'static str return karke fix kar sakte hain. &'static str string literals ko refer karta hai, jinki 'static lifetime hoti hai, matlab woh program ke duration tak rehti hain. Yahan aapke code ka updated version hai:

fn fizzbuzz(n: usize) -> &'static str {
    match (n % 3, n % 5) {
        (0, 0) => "fizzbuzz",
        (0, _) => "fizz",
        (_, 0) => "buzz",
        _ => {
            let s = n.to_string();
            Box::leak(s.into_boxed_str())
        }
    }
}

Dhyan dein ki last arm ek &'static str return karta hai jo Box::leak function ka use karke String se create kiya gaya hai, jo ek Box<T> ki ownership leta hai aur uske contents ka pointer return karta hai, effectively use leak kar deta hai. Yeh safe hai kyunki returned pointer program ke duration tak rahega, lekin ise care ke saath use karna chahiye kyunki yeh automatically clean up nahi hoga.

response_en:
Merge sort is a divide and conquer algorithm that works by recursively splitting the input array in half until the arrays are split into single elements. Then, the sorted arrays are merged back together in sorted order.

The key benefits of merge sort over a simpler algorithm like bubble sort are:

1. It is a stable sort that does not change the relative order of elements with equal keys.
2. It has an efficiency of O(n log n) time complexity, making it a relatively efficient algorithm even for large input sizes. Bubble sort, on the other hand, has a quadratic time complexity of O(n^2), making it very inefficient for larger inputs.
3. It is a generic algorithm that can sort any type of data for which a comparison function exists.

Here is a Rust implementation example:
```rust
fn merge_sort(vec: &mut [i32]) {
    if vec.len() < 2 {
        return;
    }

    let mid = vec.len() / 2;
    let (left, right) = vec.split_at_mut(mid);

    merge_sort(left);
    merge_sort(right);

    merge(left, right, vec);
}

fn merge(left: &mut [i32], right: &mut [i32], vec: &mut [i32]) {
    let mut i = 0;
    let mut j = 0;
    let mut k = 0;

    while i < left.len() && j < right.len() {
        if left[i] < right[j] {
            vec[k] = left[i];
            i += 1;
        } else {
            vec[k] = right[j];
            j += 1;
        }
        k += 1;
    }

    while i < left.len() {
        vec[k] = left[i];
        i += 1;
        k += 1;
    }

    while j < right.len() {
        vec[k] = right[j];
        j += 1;
        k += 1;
    }
}
```

romanized_response:
Merge sort ek divide and conquer algorithm hai, jo input array ko recursively aadhe mein split karke kaam karta hai, jab tak arrays single elements mein split na ho jaayen. Phir sorted arrays ko sorted order mein wapas merge kar diya jata hai.

Bubble sort jaise simple algorithm ke mukable merge sort ke key benefits ye hain:

1. Ye ek stable sort hai, jo equal keys wale elements ke relative order ko change nahi karta.
2. Iski efficiency O(n log n) time complexity hoti hai, isliye ye large input sizes ke liye bhi relatively efficient algorithm hai. Dusri taraf, bubble sort ki quadratic time complexity O(n^2) hoti hai, jiski wajah se ye bade inputs ke liye bahut inefficient ho jata hai.
3. Ye ek generic algorithm hai, jo kisi bhi type ke data ko sort kar sakta hai, agar uske liye comparison function exist karta ho.

Yahan ek Rust implementation example hai:
```rust
fn merge_sort(vec: &mut [i32]) {
    if vec.len() < 2 {
        return;
    }

    let mid = vec.len() / 2;
    let (left, right) = vec.split_at_mut(mid);

    merge_sort(left);
    merge_sort(right);

    merge(left, right, vec);
}

fn merge(left: &mut [i32], right: &mut [i32], vec: &mut [i32]) {
    let mut i = 0;
    let mut j = 0;
    let mut k = 0;

    while i < left.len() && j < right.len() {
        if left[i] < right[j] {
            vec[k] = left[i];
            i += 1;
        } else {
            vec[k] = right[j];
            j += 1;
        }
        k += 1;
    }

    while i < left.len() {
        vec[k] = left[i];
        i += 1;
        k += 1;
    }

    while j < right.len() {
        vec[k] = right[j];
        j += 1;
        k += 1;
    }
